WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:01.429
Okay.

00:00:01.429 --> 00:00:04.700
Let's go next to our caller from the seven
nine four, seven nine area code.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.319
Who's calling today from four seven nine.

00:00:07.319 --> 00:00:09.850
Oh, David, my name is Jacob.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:11.000
I'm from Fort Smith, Arkansas.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.070
How you doing today?

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:14.110
Hey, how are you doing Jacob?

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:15.110
I'm doing all right.

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:20.250
So I called you a couple of years ago and
I'm wondering if you're a, I asked you this

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:25.160
specific question and I'm just wondering if
you, you probably may not remember the question,

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.770
but like I'm wondering if you will probably
evolved on this question that I'm going to

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:30.770
ask you.

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:33.590
Basically I actually about Medicare for all
right.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:38.360
And if you think it'll ever be implemented,
but you know, with Bernie Sanders running

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:44.450
to his campaign the way he does and let's
say he does win and uh, the way he plans on

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:50.090
trying to get implemented, you think it's
more likely now, do you think you have evolved

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:56.379
on if he wins that, you know, in the next
five, 10 years we could actually have like

00:00:56.379 --> 00:01:01.380
a medical medicare for all system or do you
think it still, cause you said before, back

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:06.940
when I talked to you, it was like a state
by state issue that you probably think is

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.110
more likely.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:15.020
But like with the way Bernie Sanders is, Troy,
that rally, uh, is uh, people that his constituents

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.470
or the democratic base.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:22.290
I mean I'm just thinking it's more likely
he could possibly get it implemented, but

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:23.549
I don't know.

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:27.940
Yeah, I think that now my perspective is that
it's more likely that there will be a national

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:32.030
medicare for all implementation than I thought
whenever you call it a couple of years ago.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:36.340
I still think that there are just significant
barriers and I think that the idea of doing

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:41.840
it at the state level still at least initially
doing state single payer is still not a bad

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.710
idea, particularly because the state's most
likely to first implement it are the states

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:50.060
where the math will be most favorable to actually
doing it.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:54.520
But I've actually, the way I've evolved is
a different one on medicare for all.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:02.390
I am more open than ever to other ways of
achieving universal coverage in a nonprofit

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:03.790
non predatory way.

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:07.400
And this has become a reality from you just
by learning more about other systems, whether

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:10.999
it's the German system, French or UK, Canada,
et Cetera.

00:02:10.999 --> 00:02:15.900
So I'm a huge supporter of expanding Medicare
to medicare for all, it would be multiple

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.319
times better than what we have.

00:02:17.319 --> 00:02:20.889
But I also, and I said this on the Joe Rogan
program and it was misquoted.

00:02:20.889 --> 00:02:26.659
Interestingly, um, medicare as a base has
some issues.

00:02:26.659 --> 00:02:28.650
Of course they are solvable issues.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:35.739
So my position is I'm open to any path that
takes the vulture capitalism out of healthcare

00:02:35.739 --> 00:02:40.529
and it makes sure that everybody has access
to care regardless of ability to pay.

00:02:40.529 --> 00:02:43.449
I will have a conversation about any of those
systems.

00:02:43.449 --> 00:02:46.980
And I think Medicare for all is a good option,
but it's just one option.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:47.980
Yes.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:54.469
And I agree with you there because like with
people that are not able to afford it, I mean

00:02:54.469 --> 00:02:58.890
it just makes sense that you know, when you
need help you should be able to get it without

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:04.349
having to um, you know, break the bank, you
know, I mean any type of, I think Medicare

00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:09.199
for all would be good and you know, cause
they're always going to be rich people and

00:03:09.199 --> 00:03:13.409
poor people no matter what kind of, especially
living in a capitalist society, there's always

00:03:13.409 --> 00:03:18.120
going to be somebody at the bottom of the
ladder that they're going to need help if

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:19.120
they said people get sick.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.300
So I do agree with you.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.300
So.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.300
All right, very good.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:23.300
Appreciate the call.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:24.300
You're out.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:25.300
Audio sounding a bit odd.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:26.300
So I'm going to let you go.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:30.479
But yes, my view, if it has evolved, it has
evolved to only in being more optimistic.

00:03:30.479 --> 00:03:30.890
For sure.

